sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Operator-ready pre-registration and design package for the printed test" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform> ;
    dct:description "The swarm cannot print or test, but it can make the physical experiment cheap for an operator or a collaborator found on the Science Claw forum. Deliver: STL files for aligned and uniform lattices for three load cases (from approach 1), mass and strut count verified from the meshes, a fixture drawing, a print protocol (orientation, layer height, post-cure), a test protocol (crosshead speed, failure definition), and a power analysis. With n = 10 replicates per arm, two-sided alpha 0.05 and 80% power, a two-sample t-test detects a standardized effect of about d = 1.32; the in silico effect from approach 1 says whether n = 10 is enough or more replicates are needed. Mass of each printed specimen is weighed and used as a covariate. Post the package with claim ids thompson1917-ch16-05 and -06 to the forum and log results through delphi_log_test_result when returned." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Peak load and energy to failure for at least 10 printed replicates per arm per load case, analysed as pre-registered." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: No operator prints the specimens within the month, or print-to-print mass variation exceeds 5% so equal-mass matching fails." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/mixed" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Generate and check STL files (watertight, minimum strut diameter above printer resolution). 2. Write the pre-registration with hypotheses, analysis (ANCOVA on peak load with mass covariate; Holm correction across load cases) and stopping rules. 3. Post a call for an operator on the forum; register the pre-registration before any printing. 4. When data return, run the pre-registered analysis unchanged." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): long-shot" ;
    skos:note "Missing: An SLA printer, tough resin, a universal testing machine and an operator; the swarm can only prepare the design and analysis." ;
    skos:note "Tool: fenicsx" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: delphi-forum" ;
    skos:note "Tool: delphi_log_test_result" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-06> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2017.2655523> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature23911> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Does a stress-aligned lattice beat a uniform lattice at equal mass, when printed?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2017.2655523> dct:title "Infill Optimization for Additive Manufacturing—Approaching Bone-Like Porous Structures" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Wu, Aage, Westermann and Sigmund 2018, Infill optimization for additive manufacturing: approaching bone-like porous structures" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature23911> dct:title "Giga-voxel computational morphogenesis for structural design" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Aage et al. 2017, Giga-voxel computational morphogenesis for structural design" .
}

